@startuml Composite-BOM-Problem
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
title THE PROBLEM — bill of materials WITHOUT Composite\n(package dev.kaldiroglu.composite.bom.problem)\nNo common type, so the clients carry the recursion

package "problem — the naive design" {

  class Part {
    - partNumber : String
    - name : String
    - unitCost : Money
    - weightGrams : int
  }
  note bottom of Part
    Data only.
    No operations —
    they cannot live here.
  end note

  class Assembly {
    - partNumber : String
    - name : String
    - assemblyCost : Money
    - assemblyWeightGrams : int
    - parts : List<Part>
    - subAssemblies : List<Assembly>
    + parts() : List<Part>
    + subAssemblies() : List<Assembly>
    + addPart(p : Part) : void
    + addPart(p : Part, qty : int) : void
    + addSubAssembly(a : Assembly) : void
  }
  note bottom of Assembly
    TWO child collections,
    because no type covers both.

    Every walk must remember
    to visit both.

    addPart(p, 32) adds the SAME
    part 32 times — quantity has
    nowhere to live.
  end note

  class Service {
    - partNumber : String
    - name : String
    - fee : Money
  }
  note bottom of Service
    Added later.

    Assembly cannot even hold
    one: it would need a THIRD
    collection, and every walk
    would need a third loop.
  end note
}

package "the clients — one copy of the walk each" {

  class NaiveCosting <<static>> {
    + {static} totalCost(node : Object) : Money
    + {static} partCount(node : Object) : int
  }
  note bottom of NaiveCosting
    walk #1 and walk #2

    if (node instanceof Part) ...
    else if (node instanceof Assembly) ...
    else throw

    Parameter is Object, so the
    compiler cannot help at all.
  end note

  class NaiveShipping <<static>> {
    + {static} totalWeightGrams(node : Object) : int
    + {static} estimate(node : Object) : Money
  }
  note bottom of NaiveShipping
    walk #3 — the same recursion
    a third time, written by a
    different team months later
    because there was nothing
    to reuse.
  end note
}

Assembly *--> "parts\n*" Part
Assembly *--> "subAssemblies\n*" Assembly

NaiveCosting ..> Assembly : instanceof Part,\nelse instanceof Assembly,\nelse throw
NaiveShipping ..> Assembly : the same chain again
NaiveCosting ..> Service : throws — unknown type

note as CostNote
  <b>What this design costs</b>
  1. No common supertype → operations become
     static functions over Object.
  2. The same recursion exists three times.
  3. Two collections to keep in step.
  4. No quantities → 85 list entries per query,
     and two separate wheel objects that can
     silently drift apart.
  5. A new kind of item breaks every client.

  Compare Composite-BOM-Solution.png:
  one type, one walk, 13 line entries,
  one wheel object.
end note

@enduml
